steps.body:
Two pointers start at opposite ends and walk toward each other. `left` begins at 0, `right` at the last index, and the loop runs `while (left < right)`.

```javascript
let left = 0;
let right = arr.length - 1;
while (left < right) {
  // compare or swap arr[left] and arr[right]
  left++;
  right--;
}
```

Swap two items with the same destructuring swap you used on two variables: `[arr[left], arr[right]] = [arr[right], arr[left]];`. Because the line starts with `[`, the statement before it must end with a semicolon, or JavaScript reads the two lines as one.

Swapping the outer pair and moving inward reverses the array in place, the same thing `reverse()` does. It takes about half as many steps as there are items. With an odd length the middle item meets itself, `left < right` is false, and it stays put. Using `<=` would just swap it with itself.

The same walk checks whether an array reads the same both ways (a palindrome). Start with `true`, and on the first pair that differs set it to `false` and `break`. Comparing with `===` works item by item, but remember that `arr1 === arr2` on whole arrays compares references.

example.code:
const nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];

let left = 0;
let right = nums.length - 1;
while (left < right) {
  [nums[left], nums[right]] = [nums[right], nums[left]];
  left++;
  right--;
}
console.log(`Reversed: ${nums.join(", ")}`);

const values = [3, 7, 1, 7, 3];
let start = 0;
let end = values.length - 1;
let isPalindrome = true;
while (start < end) {
  if (values[start] !== values[end]) {
    isPalindrome = false;
    break;
  }
  start++;
  end--;
}
console.log(`Palindrome: ${isPalindrome}`);

