steps.body:
To visit **every pair** once, start the inner loop at `i + 1`. Pairs never repeat (no `7 + 2` after `2 + 7`) and an item never pairs with itself.

```javascript
for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
  for (let j = i + 1; j < arr.length; j++) {
    // arr[i] and arr[j]
  }
}
```

For **neighbor pairs**, read `arr[i]` and `arr[i + 1]` and stop one early with `i < arr.length - 1`. Without that, the last pass reads `arr[arr.length]`, which is `undefined`, and any math with it gives `NaN`.

A **sliding window** is a run of `k` items in a row. Sum the first `k` items, then slide one step at a time: add the item that enters and subtract the one that leaves.

```javascript
for (let i = k; i < arr.length; i++) {
  windowSum += arr[i] - arr[i - k];
}
```

This touches each item at most twice instead of re-adding all `k` items for every window. It assumes the array holds at least `k` items. A window of size 2 is the same as a neighbor pair.

example.code:
const nums = [2, 7, 4, 5, 1, 8];
const target = 9;
const k = 3;

console.log(`Pairs that sum to ${target}:`);
for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
  for (let j = i + 1; j < nums.length; j++) {
    if (nums[i] + nums[j] === target) {
      console.log(`${nums[i]} + ${nums[j]}`);
    }
  }
}

let rises = 0;
for (let i = 0; i < nums.length - 1; i++) {
  if (nums[i + 1] > nums[i]) {
    rises++;
  }
}
console.log(`Rises between neighbors: ${rises}`);

let windowSum = 0;
for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) {
  windowSum += nums[i];
}
let largest = windowSum;
for (let i = k; i < nums.length; i++) {
  windowSum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];
  if (windowSum > largest) {
    largest = windowSum;
  }
}
console.log(`Largest sum of ${k} in a row: ${largest}`);

